prompt:
Below is an English argument split into sentences (with roles), a glossary of
predicate senses, and for each sentence the formal statement(s) someone wrote
for it, shown as deterministic English paraphrases (sense names appear in the
predicate names, e.g. ready_packing_completed). Some sentences were instead
WAIVED, and some were OMITTED (left without any formalisation). The glossary
comes from the dossier; any "author_declared" entries were written by the
author and are not part of the dossier.

For each unit judge fidelity only:
- A formalised sentence is "faithful" if the paraphrase(s) together say what the
  sentence says: same entities, same conditions and conclusions, same
  direction of every 'if ... then', nothing added and nothing dropped. When a
  given sentence names a sense, the predicate must be that sense. When a claimed
  sentence leaves a word's sense open, the chosen sense must be one that the
  sentence's own stated reasons (via the given sentences) could establish; if
  several could, any of them is acceptable. For a sentence that states
  a conclusion with reasons ("because X, Y"), formalising the conclusion Y is
  faithful; the reasons may appear elsewhere.
- A formalisation may split one sentence into several statements; judge them
  together. A statement marked "also anchored to" other sentences may state
  several sentences jointly: it is faithful for THIS sentence only if it states
  this sentence's content correctly; a statement that states only some other
  sentence's conclusion does not formalise this one (unfaithful).
- "nodes" is the COMPLETE statement graph: every node (N1, N2, ...), its
  kind (assumption or step), its statement, the sentences it is anchored to
  (none for an unanchored helper the author added) and, for a step, the nodes
  it is derived from. Formalisation entries cite their node id. Use the graph
  to see how each step was actually derived, through any helper nodes.
- A formalised unit may list "reasons": the sentences its steps' derivations
  rest on (following unanchored helpers down to anchored nodes). Judge whether
  they are the reasons this sentence itself gives (or the given records those
  reasons rest on); a step derived from a reason the sentence does not give,
  directly or through a helper, is "unfaithful". An unanchored helper that
  states something no given sentence says makes every step resting on it
  "unfaithful".
- Part of a sentence may be inexpressible in the glossary (for example which
  sample came from which lot). A formalisation that states every expressible
  part is faithful; it is unfaithful if it drops or changes an expressible
  part.
- A waiver or omission unit shows an allowlist CODE with a fixed description.
  It is "acceptable" only if the sentence really is of that kind, and
  "unacceptable" if it asserts something the stated reasoning relies on. An
  omitted sentence with no code is "unacceptable".
Use "unfaithful"/"unacceptable" when something is wrong, "unclear" only when
the sentence itself is too ambiguous to judge.

Return {"units": [{"unit": "<unit id>", "verdict": "faithful|unfaithful|unclear|acceptable|unacceptable",
"reason": "<one sentence>"}]} with exactly one entry per unit id listed.


